WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.140
WWF WrestleMania, featuring Hulk Hogan and other WWF superstars.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:29.980
NOSTALGIA And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.580
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.700
I'm Joe.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.300
And I'm Sam.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.300
Guys, welcome to WrestleMania.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.320
Thank you. I don't know why they welcome you to it, but that's like a thing, at least in the early years.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.320
I don't know if they still do it anymore, but like it would always be like a big thing like,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.040
and welcome to WrestleMania.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.920
And it made me think of the podcast where it was like, and welcome to NEStalgia.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.440
So in a lot of ways, Nostalgia is like the WWF.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.180
Do you think that they ripped us off with our intro?

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.420
Possible I ripped them off actually, so we should probably drop it.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.820
Hmm. Okay.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.640
Well, we started talking about games from 1985, and this game is 1989, so we came first.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.200
We preceded them. But WrestleMania started in 1985.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.580
The first WrestleMania was in 1985.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.480
Oh, that's right. We were all in that same, it was like the Pixar meeting.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.880
We decided, we came up with Nostalgia and WrestleMania in the same meeting.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.720
Same meeting.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.680
We can get rid of it.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.900
We can just start blurting out like, Nostalgia here, and then continue.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.780
Yeah, I forgot that Vince McMahon was actually an original member of the Nostalgia podcast

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.120
and then went on to just do the WWF instead.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.840
He kind of was like, see you later guys.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.360
That's right. He did a Dave Mustaine on us.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.860
Yeah, if you can get a hold of episode zero of the podcast, he's still on.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.090
But I do think that I'm the only one who really knows anything about 1989 and the WWF.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.640
Am I correct? Did you guys watch old wrestling?

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.820
Like the wrestling before you were even born?

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.700
I didn't even watch wrestling after I was born.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.060
Yeah. I mean, I watched a few episodes of Dark Side of the Ring, and that's pretty much all I know

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.040
about the WWF slash E slash Z slash whatever.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.220
I watched when I was pretty young, but it still would have been like the mid-90s.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.480
So it was like the Stone Cold era and stuff.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.340
So I know who some of these people are, but I never watched any of this old stuff.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.520
Well, I would hope you know who Hulk Hogan is.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.420
Yeah. WWF really wants you to know.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.020
It's featuring Hulk Hogan and then some other schmoes.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.320
Yeah. Like Goldberg.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:27.520
Like Andre the Giant.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.560
The lovable star of Princess Bride.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.060
Do you associate him with Princess Bride more than you would with wrestling?

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.820
Just out of curiosity because you didn't watch wrestling, I'm just wondering.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.600
Yes. Wow, that's crazy.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.060
Because even if I saw him in Princess Bride, I'd think like, you know, it's no different

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.960
than in seeing the big show in Jingle All The Way.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.140
It's kind of like, of course that guy's a wrestler.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:49.740
The big show.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.480
Anyway, 1989 in wrestling.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.540
This would have been building up to WrestleMania V.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.280
As I mentioned, the first WrestleMania was in 1985.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:08.520
And the mega powers, as they were known, Macho Man, Randy Savage, and Hulk Hogan, they ended

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.640
a year long friendship.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:15.080
And now the mega powers were finally going to explode at WrestleMania V.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:20.480
However, that has very little to do with this game because this game chose to do like a

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.300
tournament mode as the main feature of the game.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:28.940
And that's an interesting take because WrestleMania IV, the previous year, was the one that had

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.200
a very long tournament.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.460
And I think critics mostly panned it.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.480
And I think even fans were like, wow, four hours of WrestleMania.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.660
Like it was really long and just dragged on and you watched a bunch of people fight each

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:45.100
other. But it's notable because our boy, Macho Man Randy Savage, finally won his first WWF title.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.600
And I am a huge Macho Man fan.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:48.520
You better believe it.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:54.620
I'd say that if I had to be a fan of a wrestler that I never really watched, it would probably

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:55.900
be Macho Man Randy Savage.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.460
Mine would be Andre the Giant.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.020
But you wouldn't pick Andre the Giant if he endorsed Slim Jims.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.880
You would pick him because of the Princess Bride.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.280
I have no relationship with Andre the Giant.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.560
I identify with Macho Man Randy Savage.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:20.520
I pick Andre because I heard that in Princess Bride, he's French and he didn't know any English.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.660
So he would just say all of his lines phonetically.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.460
Oh, I didn't know that.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.320
That just sounds really funny to me.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.060
I don't know who I would have picked, but it could have been Macho Man Randy Savage before

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.140
I played this game.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.120
But I gotta say I wasn't impressed with him in this game.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.500
It took him off my list of contenders.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.260
He's got great moves from the top rope though, Joe.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.520
He didn't get any off on me.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:47.320
Okay, that's true too.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.920
And I'm sure we're all dying to talk about the gameplay here.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:05:03.000
But I feel like if this game was made for no other reason than the pause sound, then this is Joe's shining moment to talk about the pause sound in this game.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:07.840
Because as soon as I paused this game for the first time, I knew that we would have to talk about this.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:08.820
And I don't want to wait 40 something minutes.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.080
This is crazy. You're going to lead off with the pause sound?

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.380
I am going to have plenty to say about the pause sound.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.160
I have plenty to say about the pause sound.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.140
It's having the cherry on top of the dessert before eating the appetizer.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:26.220
Well, actually, what I'm thinking now, I appreciate you teeing that up because I am going to say a lot about the pause sound and then I'm just going to sign off for the rest of the episode so you guys can take care of the rest.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.980
I did notice the pause sound.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.660
It is the longest and most intricate pause sound we've had so far.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:41.320
To the point where you can pause the game and then unpause the game and continue playing the game and the pause sound will still be playing from when you paused it.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:48.520
A little, maybe, I don't know, can we even play the pause sound here so people know what we're talking about?

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.140
Absolutely. Yeah, I will play it right now.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:53.620
Wasn't that beautiful?

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:14.200
It's a full song. I even got to the point where I couldn't figure out how to pin anyone so I had on my laptop, which was across the room, I had the instruction manual and I would pause it, run over to the instruction manual and as I'm still running over across the other side of the room, sitting down, reading the instruction manual, the pause sound is still playing across the room.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.040
So, notable.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.220
So you had to go and check every time you wanted to pin somebody?

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.000
No, it was the one time I wanted to pin somebody.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.380
Oh, oh. But it also doesn't sound like a pause sound.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.660
It actually sounds like something is starting, right?

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.100
You don't think of it as like an interruption.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.460
Right. Yeah, yeah.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.740
It feels like, I don't even know, is the game paused yet at first?

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.000
I'm like, there's so much going on.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:41.920
It just sounds like I, yeah, like I'm summoning something or something like that.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:43.660
I don't know what I'm talking about.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.680
Well, Joe, you mentioned that you had a little trouble figuring out how to pin people.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:56.100
Do you feel like, and this goes to everyone, did you feel like you had a little trouble figuring out how to do much of anything in this game at the start of it at least?

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:02.200
I would say that, you know, this game isn't impossible to play, but it certainly felt like it when I first got in the ring.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.480
I was almost just like, alright, so does Macho Man only attack sometimes?

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.860
Like, does he not attack every time I press a button?

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:11.900
I felt a little weird about things at first.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.880
Sean, I don't know if you want to talk more about that.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.680
Yeah, things feel a little sticky.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:26.740
Like, there's some lead up to the actual action that you perform before you press the, like, after you press the button to do it, which, I mean, makes sense.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:32.080
It's like, you know, wrestling games usually have that extra weight added, kind of.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:47.460
But another thing that's weird about it is, while you can walk around in, like, a form of 3D space in the ring, you only get to attack on, like, a flat plane.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:53.900
So I think that contributes to it, too, of things either feeling like they're not connecting when they should.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:08:00.040
And, yeah, it's sort of an issue that has cropped up in games like this before.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:09.620
Yeah, that was my biggest qualm, was the fact that you can walk into Z space, but you can't do anything while facing that way.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:19.260
Like, they made it possible so you can face both forward and backward, but you're useless until you're facing, like, horizontally towards your opponent, which feels like half the game is missing.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:31.540
Yeah, I would get in a lot of situations also where I would be kind of stuck in a corner and someone would just bully me because the second I move up to try and get out of the corner, I can't...

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.140
It's not like you can attack that way.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.940
So I would just get punched back into the corner.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.500
Yeah, it feels like sometimes you gotta take the hit.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.680
You know, you're like, alright, I'm just screwed here.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.320
Like, hit me so this way I can get out of where I am.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.800
And then I can try to attack you when I get back up.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:53.140
And it's also possible to cheese that on the computer if you're playing somebody low enough on a totem pole.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:09:00.680
Like, I remember in one of my matches I just sort of did that to the guy to get him down to zero energy.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.340
So, you know, you can live and die by it.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:15.840
But there are movesets in the game, and even if things rely on some kind of context, it is cool to see that only certain characters can go off the top rope because that was not something everyone used to do.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:20.440
It used to be exclusively to, like, technical wrestlers who were good at that stuff.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:22.000
That's why I mentioned Macho Man earlier on.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.480
And he was a guy who went to the turnbuckle often.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:32.020
But then you have, like, you know, Bam Bam Bigelow able to do his cartwheel and be silly like that.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.240
So I think, like, that's funny too.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.680
And it's nice that they included those things depending on the character.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:40.140
It's also, like, Hulk Hogan being the only one who can slam Andre the Giant.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:41.020
Like, that's neat.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:45.940
It first happened at WrestleMania 3, so it's even like a WrestleMania callback there.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.620
Although I'm pretty sure he also slammed Andre a few other times in non-televised events.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:52.080
But they don't want you to know about that.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.420
So this has the deep lore built into the gameplay, I see.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:08.020
Exactly. There's a lot of – you can actually feel the tension more when you're playing as Hulk versus Andre because you just know so much more context.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.660
Or if you're playing Hulk versus Macho Man because it's like that's the feud right there.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:18.320
So you feel it. When I play Hulk versus Honky Tonk Man, I'm just looking for the guitar power-up.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.860
I didn't – I've never heard of Honky Tonk Man.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.260
But he was in there.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.180
I know. But I'd heard of, like, the rest of them for the most part.

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:32.420
Honky Tonk Man was like an Elvis impersonator style thing.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.640
And, you know, each character in the game gets, like, these weird health power-ups.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:38.780
Honky Tonk Man just happens to look like a guitar.

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.800
That's the power-up I was referring to.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:44.260
But then you have Hulk Hogan's, and that looks like a crucifix.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.080
Like, what is that about?

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.620
I thought it was, like, a sword, but, yeah, in the manual it just looks like a cross.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:52.400
I thought it was an X, just the letter X.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:56.640
None of those things would make – it doesn't matter if it's a crucifix, a sword, or an X.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.900
I don't think of any of those things for Hulk.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.940
I would think, like, you know, get an American flag or something like that.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.480
The guy was a real American.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.260
Or, like, mustache wax.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:09.800
Yeah, mustache wax is good too.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:15.740
Yeah. Right? Or, you know, get Mr.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.100
T. Mr. T could be his power-up.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.140
Well, then they'd have to get the rights for Mr.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:27.540
T's likeness. Well, they wrestled together in WrestleMania 1, so I'm just trying to do as many throwbacks to the previous WrestleMania's before 5.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:35.840
Or maybe it could be, like, the title card for the reality show he'd have later in life.

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:37.760
Yeah, that's true too.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.060
What was that reality show called?

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:41.220
I can't remember now. But it got him in a lot of trouble.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:42.700
I don't remember. Oh, yeah.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.380
That show really killed him for the most part, actually.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:48.400
It was – not many good things came out of that show.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:51.660
But where is anything else in this game?

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:52.720
Where are the crowds?

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.040
Where's the atmosphere?

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.560
Where's, like, the announcers at the announcer's table?

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.560
Maybe some introductions as they enter the ring because that's, like, a big deal.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.260
Where are these things?

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.560
I will say I think that the crowd missing is definitely very strange.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:12.960
The other stuff, it's like, okay, maybe they couldn't – you know, they didn't – it's a lot to add that stuff in.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.120
But they're not even a crowd.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.160
They're just in a black void.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.300
I feel like that's just – you know, that's just a texture you can lay behind it.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:19.600
It's a little strange.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:29.780
Since we're still in the pandemic, I just want to say that, like, wrestling in WWF WrestleMania on the NES is very similar to what wrestling looks like right now on TV.

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:31.120
There's no crowd.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.160
It's just silent.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:33.960
I was thinking that. It's crazy.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:40.660
But, like, they shouldn't – I bet they weren't thinking, like, oh, how are we going to solve this whole, like, no crowd thing during the pandemic?

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:45.160
Oh, well, I once played WWF WrestleMania, and people seemed to like that game back then.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.200
So we can just simulate that but in real life.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:13:04.740
This is off topic of the game, but I think what would have been cool for plague wrestling would be if they just, like, superimposed the canned animations of, like, you know, 3D models from the games, like, just into the stands.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.420
I think that would have been a really interesting way to view wrestling.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.280
Or they could have shot the whole thing like a movie.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.520
Like, every fight is actually just, like, pre-shot and looks like a movie.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.840
A bad movie, but it would look like one, you know?

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.200
So this way you don't have to do the whole live thing and worry about that.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:37.120
And the reason I bring that up is not just to talk about regular wrestling, but what if I told you that there was another game that came out this same year on DOS computers where you could fight as Hulk Hogan and Macho Man and such.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:45.120
But instead of actually, like, going around in the ring and controlling your character and, you know, choosing their attacks and everything like that.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:55.380
Instead, you pick from a list of attacks and then it shows you, like, an image based on an actual, like, photograph of the WWF.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:14:02.940
Like, it shows you an image of the result based on, like, the computer determining how, you know, like, whether or not your move was successful.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:04.960
Like, a storybook mode.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:13.820
So this is sort of, like, an even lower, like, lower interaction version of, like, the NES Pro Wrestling?

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.820
Exactly. A choose-your-own-adventure style wrestling.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:24.000
That's what I was going to say. Yeah. Where, yeah, all you get is these, like, text fields that say, you know, I'm going to throw a punch or I'm going to climb the top rope.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:30.720
And then the game calculates, like, the probability of that working out for you and then shows an image based on the result.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:31.940
Not even, like, a video file.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:34.380
D&D. Yeah, D&D wrestling.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.280
I kind of dig it. I think that's cool.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:43.340
I think that it's, like, it's novel enough that it's, like, its own thing without trying to be a recreation of something else.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.480
You know? Does that make sense?

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:47.120
It's just interesting because they both came out in the same year.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:54.160
You know, that's why I'm calling it out because I actually think I'd prefer, in this case, just being into, like, actual video games.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:15:06.080
I prefer this WWF WrestleMania NES take here because I do like the idea of, like, I am Hulk Hogan rather than, like, I'm Hulk Hogan's manager calling the shots from the corner and then seeing if he does them or not.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.320
Speaking of the manager.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.540
I think they both kind of have their own, they both have their own, like, they would both have their own fan base.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:14.960
You know? It's like almost totally different styles of games.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:21.520
Yeah. But now that you mention the managers, like, they're all listed, like, in the game and in the manual.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:24.200
Like, why don't we get to see the managers on the sides, too?

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:31.180
And that you're missing a lot of flavor, like, in addition to, you know, crowds and that basic stuff.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:38.820
Yeah, yeah. And like you said with the managers, like, even just the intros, like, just having them come out with their manager or something would have been nice.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:53.360
They didn't forget everything, though, because there is theme music for each character that I feel like either spawns when one of the theme music runs out or if you pause the game, it automatically changes to the next track when you unpause.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.660
But those themes are pretty good.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:57.140
Yeah, I liked them.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:00.460
I didn't realize that there was any logic to it.

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.440
It just kind of kept switching at random.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:07.160
Maybe it has to do with, like, who's got more energy or something.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:12.200
I'm not sure. But I didn't know that they were personalized.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:17.600
And were you surprised when you found out that Macho Man's theme song was Pomp and Circumstance?

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:22.660
No, I remember it being kind of weird.

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:29.000
But I played as him for, like, the amount of time I played the game.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:30.980
So I heard it a lot.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:38.600
Yeah. And I remember this story of this kid who was graduating high school and they started playing Pomp and Circumstance.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:40.820
And he didn't know that that was the graduation song.

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:43.740
And he was like, holy shit, Macho Man is here?

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:46.620
At my graduation?

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.280
That's funny. Anyway, back to the game.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:58.000
Back to the game. Not that, you know, having a robust selection of wrestlers is a big deal.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.840
But I do think it's nice that they didn't just make it two or even just four characters.

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:07.060
Like, if they're going to make it a tournament mode, they at least gave you eight characters to choose from.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:18.360
And I feel like they all are different enough, both in personalities from their actual sport, but also, like, in the game as well, that you can pick different people.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:23.080
I know Sean and I mostly played as Macho Man just because why wouldn't you pick Macho Man if the option is available to you?

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:27.240
However, like, it is a different experience playing as different characters.

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:28.380
I think that needs to be mentioned.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.760
It's not like everybody has the same punch.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:36.400
Everybody does the same thing when they're running off the ropes and stuff like that.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:37.960
So I think that's nice. What did you guys think of that?

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:45.240
Yeah, on one level, I was pretty impressed with how they, I mean, as someone who doesn't really know these people very well,

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:50.820
it seems like they put a lot of thought into making them feel like who they are.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:56.520
You know, making them seem like, you know, giving them, like you said about the cartwheel before and things like,

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:00.500
all their different moves kind of feeling like those, you know, their style.

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:06.360
But on the other hand, I do think that sometimes maybe they went a little overboard with, like,

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:12.400
sometimes even just to pin is a different combination of buttons, apparently, which maybe is why I was having trouble.

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:17.640
I found that the way that you pin in the manual is not, is just wrong anyways.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:18.600
I was doing it a different way.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:22.260
But why should doing the same move take different buttons for different characters?

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:24.020
So, like, there were pros and cons to me.

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:34.280
Yeah, I would say that I liked that there was more than just, like, palette swaps here, like we may have seen in previous games.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:39.780
Like, the closest that I can think of to the system that we're seeing here,

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:47.040
I don't know if it was ice hockey or if it was another one that we played around the same time,

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:55.460
another hockey game where you could make your team filled with, like, three different archetypes of player.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:57.600
Yeah, that's ice hockey. Okay, that was ice hockey.

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:02.360
And I just liked that there were, like, there were different feel,

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:04.280
that there was a different feel to each character.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:12.620
Like, if you're playing as Andrei the Giant, I did do a match with him just to say that I didn't just play as Macho Man Randy Savage.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:18.140
And he's got, he's a little slower, but he's also got, like, much more reach.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:23.180
Like, it feels like a different game, like, let alone the different moveset.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.080
It is odd that pin is different.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:28.760
Like, that seems like it should have been a universal input.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:33.820
But I liked it more than I disliked it.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:44.580
Yeah, it seems like the only reason why pin is a different moveset is because certain characters have potentially more moves than others or aren't applicable.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:53.320
Like, I think Bam Bam Bigelow can't body slam, so he doesn't have an A plus B button.

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:57.900
But he has, like, an A plus B button if you have it back to the opponent.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:07.160
So, like, again, that whole, like, being contextual to what you're doing thing seems nice on paper because there's only an A and a B button and some directional pads.

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:10.000
So, like, you kind of do need to figure out how to do this.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:23.920
And we've all agreed now that grabbing, grabbing the, going into the grapple against an enemy and then having a menu pop up with possible moves that you'd like to make is not what we want to see.

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:27.280
And that was in tag team wrestling early in 1986.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:31.740
You can check out that episode for our thoughts on a much more jank wrestling game.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:40.260
However, here, it's just, I feel like right now this is actually convincing me that we just might not see a good wrestling game on the NES.

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:43.480
And that's not because I think WWF would have figured it out on the first bout.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:49.800
But I'm just thinking about, like, the possible combinations that you could do with just two buttons and a bunch of directions.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:58.680
And a lot of it is going to rely on this context-based stuff that just, you know, every now and again is just going to get you in a bad situation.

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:11.040
Yeah. I'd say that I appreciate, I think this is the best feeling wrestling game that we've played so far.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.520
I would say that that is a low bar to clear.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:23.860
But it is slowly, I mean, I don't think they'll ever get there, but they are slowly fixing all of the faults that we see in these games.

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:35.580
I wonder if they could take, for a future game, almost like a Street Fighter, Mortal Kombat kind of approach, where, like, the input directions on the D-pad determine the moves.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:40.620
And that's, like, a way you can get other moves in there, but I don't know how jank that would be.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:49.000
I think the only reason why they haven't opted to do that yet is, one, those games haven't come out yet, and they're the ones that, like, make that whole idea even possible.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.460
Like, I don't think games were doing that before those fighting games came out.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:56.780
But also, you know, you have a sense of 3D movement in the ring.

00:21:56.780 --> 00:22:03.260
If you made this a strict 2D fighter, it wouldn't quite feel like a wrestling game.

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:08.620
It would just be more like an Urban Champion style game for the NES, you know?

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:17.300
Mm-hmm. In that case, like, for me, I feel like the more arcade-y something, a sports game feels on a retro console, the better it'll play.

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:25.840
And the fact that they're going for realism with this game, I feel like, I agree with you.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.240
I don't know if it could get, if the controls could get tighter.

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:37.600
But, you know, I'm glad you mentioned Street Fighter and those kind of games, because, in a way, this really is a fighting game, right?

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:45.060
Like, we joked about way back in Trojan, our first episode for 1987, or maybe it was our second episode, I don't remember.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:52.240
Anyway, we joked about how that has a fighting game mode, and those guys would go on to create Street Fighter, so that's pretty interesting.

00:22:52.240 --> 00:23:02.800
But here, you know, you've got an energy meter, you've got your ability to do different moves based on the context of situations.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:09.800
So, I actually think, like, this isn't, like, that far off from the idea of what fighting games would eventually become.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:20.160
It's still not, it's not there, but it's also interesting to see these proto, you know, fighting, fighting, what do you call those fighting games?

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:21.200
Like, is that what you call them?

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:22.780
Fighting games? This game's kind of weird.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:23.640
It feels wrong to me.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:30.060
MLG? It feels weird to just call Street Fighter a fighting game, though, right?

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:31.280
Like, it's gotta be something.

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.620
A 2D fighting game.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:35.940
There you go. Sure, sure, why not?

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:43.600
It just feels strange that shmups have, like, a cool category, and Street Fighter doesn't have, like, a shmup-like word for it.

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:56.220
I do think that a big reason that it feels like it's getting closer to fighting games that we know and stuff is what we already talked about, is that you have to learn your character.

00:23:56.700 --> 00:24:05.900
That's something we didn't really have so much before, and maybe a little bit, but, like, this, the pros and cons aside, like, it is, you do have to, like, learn your character to learn that character's moves.

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:14.720
And, like, you get good with one character, and I think that makes it feel a lot closer to, like, what we're more familiar with.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:15.920
Yeah, I appreciate that.

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:25.880
I didn't really think about it that way, that, like, some of the depth and the replayability of a game like this, it, like, just boils down to, like, who you select.

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:28.840
And I appreciate that.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:33.620
It sort of brings us to a more modern view of these kinds of games.

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:40.340
Again, because I keep thinking about games in the past basically just being palette swaps.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:49.640
So, while it's a weird execution, I still view it positively.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:08.480
So, did anyone actually wind up becoming the WWF Heavyweight Champion and beating the tournament mode and fighting every other character?

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:12.380
I got, like, three or four guys in at that point.

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:15.440
It just seemed even harder to land a hit.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:24.760
Like, DiBiase was a chump, but everything beyond that, it got exponentially harder for me.

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:33.600
It was on that road for me, and then I actually lost a couple times to Honky Tonk Man, because he had gotten harder.

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:41.620
So, then after Honky Tonk Man, when I finally beat him, I got to Randy Savage, and I thought, okay, well, now I'm definitely done, because it's just going to get harder.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:43.160
And he was like a cakewalk.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:45.120
And then I lost on Andre the Giant.

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:46.500
So, no, I also didn't win.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:49.040
I did not do well in this game.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.220
I'll just leave it at that.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:26:04.620
Yeah, yeah. You know, what's funny is, like, we joked about how Hulk gets all the attention and stuff like that, and you got to remember, Hulkamania was a real thing in the late 80s and even early 90s.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:07.900
The guy was more than just the WWF.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:11.240
He was in movies. He was just a big celeb in general.

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:15.520
And so, it makes sense that they would call him out on the back of the box rather than any other character.

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:27.800
But it also feels like they kind of made him, I don't want to say, like, the Mike Tyson version of this game, but they definitely made him the best character in the game and the hardest one to beat, personally.

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:34.780
So, like, playing as him was a huge advantage, but also going against him just seemed like it was a really tough fight.

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:40.420
I don't know if you guys felt like they gave him a better moveset or just made him more powerful.

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:41.820
I don't know what it was about him.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:43.240
He just felt hard to beat.

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:46.900
I didn't ever get to him.

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:56.100
So, unfortunately, while I played as him for a little bit, I did not even face the computer as Hulk.

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:00.920
And I only faced Hulk as the single-player mode.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.540
I think in tournament, he actually probably is saved for last, if I think about it.

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:06.480
He is. Yeah, because I couldn't get to him.

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:08.580
Yeah, I did the same thing.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:13.360
I just faced him. I just selected him outside of a tournament just to see what it was like, and I lost.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:16.040
I don't know if that means he was harder or I'm just bad.

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:19.680
I also had a hard time. I feel like him and Andre the Giant were both very difficult.

00:27:20.480 --> 00:27:24.120
I mean, they were all difficult, don't get me wrong, but those two seemed like they were elevated.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:56.420
And just as a side note about our boy, Macho Man, I just didn't appreciate how in the manual they kept calling him Randy Macho Man Savage.

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:58.180
It really doesn't have the same cadence.

00:27:59.240 --> 00:28:01.480
It felt kind of disrespectful.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:07.780
Maybe they called him that in the 80s, but I've only known him as Macho Man Randy Savage.

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:09.140
Can you clarify, Mike?

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:12.860
They were definitely calling him Macho Man Randy Savage.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:19.020
So that is weird and something that maybe my eyes corrected while I was reading the manual.

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:29.880
Maybe I just chose to – because, you know, and now that I'm looking at the manual, since you brought it up, I brought up my own, and it is weird to see Randy Macho Man Savage there.

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:36.520
It's also like there's Ted Million Dollar Man DiBiase, and I don't think that's right either.

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:39.940
I think it was Ted DiBiase, the Million Dollar Man.

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:43.120
Not like Million Dollar Man is like his nickname.

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:45.720
It's like he is the Million Dollar Man.

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:49.040
I see. So they're just chumps.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:55.760
Yeah. I don't know what this is about, but, you know, this is another one of those games that was made by Rare.

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:02.340
So, you know, questionable output from them after a hot start on the system.

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:06.080
They went from Slalom and –

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:07.200
Great game. Yeah.

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:08.560
What's questionable about Slalom?

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:10.140
What was that other game? Wizards and Warriors?

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:11.460
Did they make Wizards and Warriors?

00:29:11.540 --> 00:29:14.320
I feel like they did. I'll say they did, and then I'll edit it out if I'm wrong.

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:21.020
But they made those games, and now all of a sudden it's like it's okay to start making some garbage too.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:22.400
Not that this is garbage.

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:26.540
I'm just saying, like, for Rare's quality of stuff, this is a step down.

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:27.620
Let's be real. Yeah.

00:29:27.740 --> 00:29:29.700
There's no big butts in this game like Slalom.

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:33.900
Right. I don't remember the big butts in Slalom.

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:35.500
Did we make a big joke about it?

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:36.540
I don't know how you could miss it.

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.840
It was – Yeah. It's right in your face, actually.

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:41.200
It's like the center point, the focal point of the screen.

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:43.380
That's why the game was essential for –

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:49.200
All right. Well, we got a few more WWF games to go on the NES.

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:55.100
I don't know the titles of all of them off the top of my head, but I do know that the very next one is going to be a WrestleMania game again.

00:29:55.100 --> 00:30:00.500
It is WWF WrestleMania Challenge, and if I had to guess, it's probably made by Rare as well.

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:07.560
Hope to be wrong, but I'd like to see each third-party developer get a stab at making a WWF game.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:08.620
What do you got, Konami?

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:11.720
Capcom, what do you bring in to the WWF genre?

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:13.540
I'd love to see it. LJN, you too.

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:19.360
Why not? We're probably not going to get that many, but I think we get at least four of these guys on the NES.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:21.100
Give me a Data East one.

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:25.640
No. Give me a Jaleco version.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:29.200
I want to see how Data East says Randy Savage's name.

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:31.440
That's the test.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.280
Everybody gets the Macho Man Randy Savage test.

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:36.320
I want to see a Will Wright version.

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:38.700
Oh, that would be interesting.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:44.860
You're a manager in that game, and you have to try to sign your wrestler through –

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:49.740
first, you're in the Northeast Regional Division, and you've got to build yourself up to the WWF.

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:56.120
And then they start getting concussions, and you have to be like, all right, which one – like, how much can I push you before you're going to sue me?

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:58.840
Oh, I like that. You're managing a bunch of different wrestlers, too.

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:00.520
Don't put all of your eggs in one basket.

00:31:00.520 --> 00:31:02.620
It's like it's a wrestling manager simulator.

00:31:02.620 --> 00:31:05.100
I would play that game for 100%.

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:08.260
Absolutely. Any game with concussions, really.

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:09.740
That's why we play Madden every year.

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:22.520
I thought a Will Wright version would be more like you create like a prototype version of the sport of wrestling, and then you let it evolve over time into what we know as wrestling today.

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:23.780
Like spore?

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:34.700
Yeah. It feels more like a post-show thing, but like someone has to explain to me the origins of staged wrestling in general.

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:43.780
Like how did it just become a thing that there are now like worldwide leagues for this, and it's just globally accepted that nobody actually wrestles anybody for real?

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:45.260
It's just bizarre.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:50.220
Not to get into it, but I mean isn't it like a circus thing?

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:53.460
Yes, it is, but I just meant like for how long?

00:31:53.580 --> 00:31:56.260
Like did the Greeks do this even way back when?

00:31:56.380 --> 00:32:02.600
Like did they have their own form of staged fighting that wasn't necessarily wrestling we know today but was still like a thing?

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:04.220
Was the Coliseum staged?

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:07.180
Did those lions really eat those guys?

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:10.980
Something tells me yes.

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:12.760
Okay. That's fair.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:16.200
But that wasn't Frank DiBiase.

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:17.680
I just like to say his name.

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:24.920
Right. I just meant like maybe it didn't happen.

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:27.560
I mean, you know, they wouldn't be here if that happened.

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:36.300
That's true. Quick shout out to WWF WrestleMania 2000, a WWF WrestleMania game I really like, and that's on the N64.

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:44.280
And also if we're talking about N64 wrestling games, you might as well bring up WWF No Mercy where you have to say dig diggity dog.

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:47.700
That's just something everybody has to say when they bring up WWF No Mercy.

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:49.700
You'll know what I'm talking about if you're a real one.

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:54.700
Also, Joe, I think you wanted to talk about WWF SmackDown on the PlayStation 1.

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:56.600
Well, I mentioned it.

00:32:56.720 --> 00:32:58.100
Did I mention it last week on the episode?

00:32:58.580 --> 00:33:06.460
I think so. That was the first WWF game that I played, and that was when I was sort of into WWF at the time.

00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:10.340
And that game, as far as I remember, was pretty great.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:13.460
I'm sure if I played it now, I'd find all kinds of flaws with it.

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:22.580
But I was hoping that this game would feel a little more like that, obviously with limited expectations.

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:29.220
Right, but that one actually had a story mode that could go on for multiple years.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:32.980
This game definitely needs more than a tournament mode.

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:37.460
It would be cool if you built up to WrestleMania, not just singular fights.

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:43.440
Right, but also on that note too, I don't know about you guys, but I wasn't able to play any one multiplayer.

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:45.520
I think that this is another game.

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:51.720
I don't think it would turn anything around or be a significantly better game in multiplayer.

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:56.480
But I feel like this is a game that is probably at least more fun in multiplayer.

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:06.300
It's not necessarily fun to go against the computer or as fun to go against the computer than it is to actually compete with someone else in any kind of wrestling game like this.

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:12.480
And that's what that old game kind of invoked for me when I was hoping to get here.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:22.740
Yeah, I don't know. I feel like I would have gotten very frustrated at playing this game with any of you because you'd be landing attacks on me that I feel like shouldn't register and I'd be cursing the whole time.

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:24.420
Don't be so hard on yourself.

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:29.400
Yeah, I don't think I'd be winning any World Heavyweight Championships against real people.

00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:32.100
My only shot here was against the computers and I couldn't even do that.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:42.080
So I don't win the belt, but maybe this game will get the championship belt in a little thing we like to call the Essential Games List.

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:48.200
In this corner, Sean the Revolution.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:51.600
Give us your Essential Games List vote.

00:34:53.680 --> 00:35:01.680
This game is not as jank as past wrestling games, but it's 100% a jank game.

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:26.940
I think that there's a lot of great steps forward in presentation, especially considering these are actually like real wrestlers with kind of, you know, they have things about the gameplay that you play as them that are somewhat based off of the wrestler themselves.

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:28.740
So I thought that was cool.

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:32.320
But playing the game is it's it's very frustrating.

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:37.320
They don't have this down yet, so it is not essential.

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:41.800
All right. You heard it first from the revolution himself.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:47.800
Yeah. Sam, would you like to vote?

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:51.080
Yes. Just Sam.

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:56.200
That's my name. End of this corner.

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:04.140
Sam. Well, you usually do a big like, you know, in our in our best of 88 episodes, you had a big like number 10.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:06.600
Like you, you usually do your own hyping.

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:07.960
So I felt like. Oh, yeah.

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:09.740
Just Sam. You could turn that into something.

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:12.940
You could be like, yeah, I'm Sam and I'm here to tell you, man.

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:15.140
Like. Yeah.

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:18.340
Um. It's not essential.

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:22.020
That's really all you have to say.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:23.580
No, no, no. I'm just kidding.

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:29.360
It's it's not essential, but it it's the best of the wrestling games that we've played so far.

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:34.940
I like that each of the characters is is like their own character and they feel different.

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:40.660
It's not just a palette swap like we've mentioned before, but there's still a lot to be.

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:43.100
There's a lot lacking in this game.

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:45.240
And that's what I have to say about that.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:46.700
All right. Brother.

00:36:47.540 --> 00:36:50.100
Yeah, brother. That's what I'm talking about, man.

00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:53.940
Joe. Just Joe.

00:36:54.460 --> 00:36:55.780
Just a guy named Joe.

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:57.620
That could be. Wait, that's the only one.

00:36:57.780 --> 00:36:59.240
I'm the only one with a nickname.

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:08.640
Come on. No, but I think just a guy named Joe is actually interesting because that's like there's a Spider-Man comic early on where like they they just said just a guy named Joe.

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:09.980
And I never quite understood that.

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:12.900
I was like, is Spider-Man the guy who's just a guy named Joe?

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:16.000
And is though like his name's Peter Parker.

00:37:16.260 --> 00:37:17.800
So why would you put that on the cover?

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:20.380
Anyway, interesting tangent there.

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:22.540
Joe's name should be Spider-Man.

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:27.500
Right. Oh, Joe's name could be Spider-Man and Spider-Man's name could be just a guy named Joe.

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:31.940
Yeah, or Spider-Man. You're blowing my secret here that I'm Spider-Man.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:33.100
Oh, whoops.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:36.700
Well, you also fought Macho Man then in Spider-Man 1, the movie.

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:38.720
That's right. Was that Macho Man?

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:40.120
That was Macho Man.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:41.100
I forgot about that.

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:42.880
That's right. Yeah.

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:44.100
Bone Saw is always ready.

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:46.760
Bone Saw. So we weren't off track at all.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:51.680
But here's my essential games thoughts on this game.

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:58.480
So I had, before I played this, I had actually seen some reviews on this just in the past.

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:01.000
And they were really, really bad.

00:38:01.420 --> 00:38:04.560
And I'm not saying that that review was not deserved.

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:08.100
But I will say that that colored my opinion in a way that this game surprised me.

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:09.880
And it was not as bad as I thought it was.

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:16.060
Especially because I was also thinking of other similar games we've played on the NES so far.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:18.680
And I do think that there's a level of charm.

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:24.500
And I do think that there's a good amount of, I don't know, there's a good amount of fan appreciation.

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:27.000
Or a good amount of attention to detail.

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:34.380
That if you were a fan in the 80s, you'd probably, and you're a kid and you haven't experienced other games better than this,

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:35.620
you'd probably like this game quite a bit.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:39.200
But that being said, it's a very flawed game.

00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:41.480
Especially just with the way it controls.

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:45.240
Where you can't fight facing forward or facing backwards.

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:48.000
And it's a little simplified.

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:50.620
And sometimes it feels like your inputs aren't working.

00:38:50.800 --> 00:38:51.840
It's definitely not essential.

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:58.240
I would say I would probably only actually give it a play it if you're into wrestling games anyways and you're interested in this.

00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:00.700
But there are definitely plenty of better options out there.

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:01.680
So no, not essential.

00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:04.580
Alright, so there's no chance of it being on the essential games list.

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:09.140
But I will throw out there that I don't think this is the best wrestling game we've had on the NES so far.

00:39:09.340 --> 00:39:15.380
I do think pro wrestling, which was actually made by Nintendo, is the best and the standard to go off of.

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:22.100
And as you know from last episode, I think I said my rules are that if a game in a genre already is better than it,

00:39:22.140 --> 00:39:24.480
but that one's not on the list, why should this one be?

00:39:24.580 --> 00:39:26.000
And that still holds true here.

00:39:26.700 --> 00:39:32.440
You know, it's a wrestling game that includes some fan service, but it missed a lot of stuff that we talked about early on.

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:35.960
Where the heck is the crowd, the atmosphere, the introductions, the announcers?

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:42.160
This stuff needs to be in there, especially if you're trying to match the presentational format of a WrestleMania.

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:46.920
You can't call your game WrestleMania where you're talking about crowds of like 93,000.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:48.100
That was all made up too.

00:39:48.100 --> 00:39:54.620
But at least they could have made up the crowds here and pretended like there were 93,000 people in attendance rather than just lie to the public.

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:56.580
However, that's not here.

00:39:56.840 --> 00:40:03.880
You know, there's really nothing else here other than the characters that, you know, I feel like are a good representation.

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:07.980
I'm a little surprised there's no Ricky the Dragon or Ultimate Warrior in this game.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:09.780
But we'll take what we can get.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:11.180
And I think this is a good start.

00:40:11.180 --> 00:40:14.260
And I'm not going to dismiss future WWF NES games.

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:17.920
That said, I know Joe mentioned that people are tough on these games.

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:19.260
Perhaps a little too tough.

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:21.040
You know, it takes some learning.

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:22.000
But you can get there.

00:40:22.520 --> 00:40:26.180
However, if we're looking at the Essential Games list as games you should go back and play today,

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:31.720
I'm saying that if you want to fight with the same set of awesome wrestlers in your roster,

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:37.580
allow me to recommend Legends of WrestleMania on like the GameCube, PS2, and Xbox,

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:41.040
where all the presentational stuff of WrestleMania is there.

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:46.740
Plus the ability to play as guys like Hulk Hogan, Macho Man, and yes, even Ultimate Warrior.

00:40:47.340 --> 00:40:49.960
So Legends of WrestleMania is the game I would recommend.

00:40:49.960 --> 00:40:55.040
I'm not saying that's going on the NES Essential Games list, but it would be on some kind of Essential Games list in a different universe.

00:40:55.040 --> 00:40:57.020
What about like Here Comes the Pain, you know?

00:40:57.220 --> 00:40:58.700
That's a good one, right?

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:00.040
It's an okay game.

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:10.680
Legends of WrestleMania embraced like the old campiness of the WWF and WrestleMania in general and stuff like that

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:12.080
and really like fit into everything.

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:16.980
So I'm saying if you're looking for like a WrestleMania game, Legends of WrestleMania is my pick.

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:22.220
And a game that would be more likely, not is, but more likely to be on my Essential Games list

00:41:22.220 --> 00:41:26.280
than whatever WWF WrestleMania poses itself to be on the NES.

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:29.480
So for that reason, it will not be joining the Essential Games list.

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:32.700
But not many games get a chance for that anyway.

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:36.800
And 1988 is proving rough for Essential Games list.

00:41:36.940 --> 00:41:38.840
Sesame Street 1, 2, 3 came close.

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:41.020
89. However, some bad menu.

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:42.340
Oh, 1989. That's right.

00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:49.480
Sesame Street 1, 2, 3 came close, but some bad NES games, you know, will be found as we continue to go through.

00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:53.880
A couple of people wrote into us and were like, hey, just so you know, games get worse as the years go on.

00:41:53.960 --> 00:41:56.000
And I'm like, don't threaten me with a bad time.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.420
Good time. No, bad time.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:00.640
They're threatening me with a bad time.

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:01.820
Oh, yes, they are. Yeah, they are.

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:05.920
But you're right. That's the usual saying is don't threaten me with a good time.

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:11.960
Another thing we should probably mention is that we're trying to plan a little game jam, nostalgia style.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:19.900
If you're not familiar with what a game jam is, we'll probably throw a link in the show notes to the actual website and everything like that or some details that we have.

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:21.920
And we'll be talking about it on Twitter, too, to build up to it.

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:29.900
But essentially, we're going to try and host something where people who are familiar with creating games can try to make some NES-style games of their own.

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:32.880
And we will play them on a special episode.

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:37.140
I know Sam, this Sam, this very same Sam.

00:42:37.400 --> 00:42:41.240
Sam. Yeah, that Sam is going to be making a game.

00:42:41.240 --> 00:42:43.620
So if he can make a game, why can't you?

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:44.720
What's your excuse?

00:42:45.520 --> 00:42:47.900
Yeah. Sam, do you want to talk a little bit more about this game jam?

00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:52.940
Go for it, Sam. I think you just about got it.

00:42:53.080 --> 00:43:01.180
But for anyone who's not familiar with game jams, it's kind of like a boot camp where you have a limited amount of time to make a game.

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:11.200
And for this one specifically, we're asking that your games are themed after the NES.

00:43:11.580 --> 00:43:24.860
So if it's some kind of mechanic from an NES game that you like or just something that you think would be cool to play on the NES, we're asking that it's like the NES-style graphics.

00:43:24.860 --> 00:43:34.760
So I think that's 256 by 240 for resolution size and audio that doesn't have to be exactly NES but sounds like NES audio.

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:43.080
To get the aesthetic. Yeah, just to try and fit the aesthetic of an NES so it shouldn't be like anything crazy in 3D or anything like that, like pixel art.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:46.540
It should be pixel art, I mean.

00:43:47.360 --> 00:43:50.380
And that's it. And I just hope that people join and have fun.

00:43:50.860 --> 00:43:51.900
Yeah, it sounds awesome.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:55.180
We'll be running it from March 19th to the 28th.

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:59.520
And if you can't make games, don't worry because you'll also get a chance to play these games.

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:01.260
You don't just have to hear about them from us.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:06.140
But if you can make games, that means now your audience is going to be larger than just four people.

00:44:06.260 --> 00:44:09.860
It's going to be every listener of Nostalgia as well.

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:12.480
And believe me, there are tens of us.

00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:23.340
That said, we'll continue to talk about this on Twitter, at NostalgiaCast if you don't know.

00:44:23.740 --> 00:44:27.120
And don't expect this game jam to be the last fun thing we do.

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:28.800
